{
  "id": "family-photos",
  "uuid": "54c750e2-e6aa-54ec-9eaa-e99e98865b34",
  "title": "Who's in the Photo? Talking Family",
  "category": "people",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "level": 2,
  "order": 202,
  "theme": "people",
  "culturalNote": "Kenyans ask about family early and warmly — \"Una familia?\" isn't nosy, it's friendly. Family words are flexible: dada and kaka (sister, brother) are also how you politely address any young woman or man, and cousins are often just called brothers and sisters. People (m/wa nouns) take their own agreement: mtoto mmoja (one child), watoto wawili (two children), mke wangu (my wife) — but everyday family words like mama, dada, nyanya take yangu: mama yangu, dada yangu.",
  "startingPoint": "Lunch break at the office. Your colleague Achieng spots the photo on your phone's lock screen.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "na-have",
    "possessives",
    "demonstratives",
    "negative-present"
  ],
  "grammar": [
